EBS

AWS EBS Notes (Elastic Block Store)


What is EBS?

	Amazon Web Services EBS (Elastic Block Store) is block storage used with EC2 instances.

Think of EBS like:

	Hard Disk for EC2

Used for:

	OS storage
	Databases
	Application files
	Persistent storage


Types of EBS Volumes


Type			Use Case
gp3				General purpose SSD
io1/io2			High-performance databases
st1				Throughput workloads
sc1				Cold storage


EBS Important Points

Feature							Description

Persistent						Data remains after reboot
AZ-specific						EBS exists in one Availability Zone
Can detach/attach				Move between instances
Snapshots						Backup to S3
Encryption						Supported
Resize							Increase size anytime

-----------------------------------------------------------

Step 1 — Create EBS Volume

	Go to:

		→ EC2 → Elastic Block Store → Volumes → Create Volume

	Configuration:

		Field				Value

		Volume Type			gp3
		Size				10 GB
		Availability Zone	ap-south-2a

	IMPORTANT:

		EBS and EC2 must be in SAME Availability Zone



	Example:

		Resource	AZ
		EC2			ap-south-2a
		EBS			ap-south-2a


What is different AZ's used for both? 


----------------------------------------------------------------------



Step 2 — Attach EBS to EC2 Instance

	After creating volume:

		→ Select Volume

		→ Actions → Attach Volume

	Configuration:

		Field		Value

		Instance	Select EC2
		Device Name	/dev/xvdf

	Example:

		/dev/xvdf


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Step 3 — Verify EBS Inside EC2

	SSH into EC2.

	Check disks:

		lsblk

Example output:

	NAME    MAJ:MIN RM SIZE RO TYPE MOUNTPOINT
	xvda    202:0    0   8G  0 disk
	└─xvda1 202:1    0   8G  0 part /

xvdf    202:80   0  10G  0 disk

Here:

	xvdf

	is new EBS volume.


-----------------------------------------------------------------------------


Step 4 — Create Filesystem

Format disk:

	sudo mkfs -t ext4 /dev/xvdf


----------------------------------------------------------------------------

Step 5 — Mount EBS Volume

	Create directory:

		sudo mkdir /data

	Mount volume:

		sudo mount /dev/xvdf /data

	Verify:

		df -h


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------


Step 6 — Make Mount Permanent

	Open fstab:

		sudo nano /etc/fstab

	Add:

		/dev/xvdf   /data   ext4   defaults,nofail   0   2

	Save and exit.

	Test:

		sudo mount -a


----------------------------------------------------------------------------


Step 7 — Create Data in EBS

	Example:

		echo "Hello EBS" | sudo tee /data/test.txt

	Check:

		cat /data/test.txt



--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Step 8 — Create Snapshot

	Go to:

		→ EC2 → Volumes

	Select volume.

		Actions → Create Snapshot



	Configuration
		Field			Value

		Name			demo-snapshot
		Description		Backup of EBS

		Click:

		Create Snapshot


-------------------------------------------------------------------------


What is Snapshot?

	Snapshot is:

		Backup of EBS volume stored in S3

	Benefits:

		Backup

			Disaster Recovery
			Clone servers
			Create new volumes

------------------------------------------------------------------------


Step 9 — Create New EBS from Snapshot

	Go to:

		→ EC2 → Snapshots

	Select snapshot.

		Actions → Create Volume from Snapshot

	Configuration
	
	Field			Value

	Volume Type		gp3
	Size			Same or Bigger
	AZ				ap-south-2a

	Click:

		Create Volume


---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Step 10 — Attach New EBS to Another EC2

	Select newly created volume.

		Actions → Attach Volume

	Configuration
		Field			Value
		Instance			Another EC2
		Device Name			/dev/xvdf


----------------------------------------------------------------------------


Step 11 — Verify Data on New EC2

	SSH into second EC2.

	Check disk:

		lsblk

	Mount volume:

		sudo mkdir /restore
		sudo mount /dev/xvdf /restore

	Verify data:

		ls /restore

	Example:

		test.txt

	Read file:

		cat /restore/test.txt

	Output:

		Hello EBS


Architecture Flow:


	EBS Volume
	    ↓
	Snapshot
	    ↓
	Create New EBS
	    ↓
	Attach to Another EC2